). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2554095b-6917-59fe-b116-930c3ba77279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345688} {:answer/id #uuid "33ec1111-7230-53ee-803c-22620a98b2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345805} {:answer/id #uuid "3c956ac0-c11e-5431-9585-446ac6c7023b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345863} {:answer/id #uuid "96b74ec0-88a6-5cc8-a484-f8197aa84eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346669} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f020df-8728-5d90-a819-fa4f38aa06f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347548}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345688} :answer-field/id #uuid "77c66077-e6a0-52cd-8409-bf48d80534b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3212ae7d-e55b-5563-bea8-a1958564eb0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79322" :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $4$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $4$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, the dimension of the span is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dcdf7ad-dc8c-5b97-9b5e-c7b134b49b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451717} {:answer/id #uuid "6c5f343f-9134-5395-8a95-5714eed53c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451718} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5ea2e4-dd11-528a-87f8-7c77094a84a7" :answer-field/key "${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452706} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "996635a3-7de7-52e1-9885-d13e9600bdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451719} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9a86f7-257f-52de-b0bc-b84e386c256a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451720} :answer-field/id #uuid "90409394-7c69-5b92-8b05-88400b81ffd6" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452707} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ed5a0f9-b208-53b1-948d-e048fe02a61a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451721} {:answer/id #uuid "f080355d-0cd5-5f51-97e4-a3f5a882e7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451722} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5f3fcac-5294-5f8b-8c36-f97bba56f78e" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452708}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1fa1e936-cb5b-5073-aaa6-f5f5e931fac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79323" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$” is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ only, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$ is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$ is true. Since ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the given span and this basis contains $3$ vectors, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w}$”; $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}})=3$; $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},w})=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85518922-a8e2-5430-be49-fc5bd9618d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451723} {:answer/id #uuid "32d23fcd-416f-5d3c-bfbd-0efc26263051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6feab46e-0164-581d-b9ac-417f088f9a3e" :answer-field/key "${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452709} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "153dcd4c-f868-5cb9-9348-c787feeb008f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451725} {:answer/id #uuid "db92ed10-887e-5be5-8e25-a589761415a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dae3873-2189-5c0f-a6cd-a4ed4645e74e" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452710} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb8f64e6-edb1-5d35-982e-d14dca05b3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451727} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ffa827-33a1-5d4c-86a0-6a5b242d8e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451727} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f7b874c-9786-5d4f-b21e-8d93eb56af6a" :answer-field/key "$\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})$ $=5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64e956c1-f323-5e83-8c0c-e6640c910184" :question/math-academy-id "q-79326" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement “${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$” is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by the vectors by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ Moreover, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$ is true. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$, and these are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})$ $=5$ is false. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}+{v}_{4}.$ Hence,$w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}.$ this means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\}$ is completely spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4},w\\})=4$ (not $5$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\}$”; $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\})=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348214 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e335be1-975b-5232-9bd4-5eac32984abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346161} {:answer/id #uuid "91458386-a13b-547c-a3af-462a321d6225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346618} {:answer/id #uuid "c25b38bf-d190-5b50-8b41-ecee957bc77c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347033} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5bfa0e-b88d-5bfd-8880-005919b2a2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347294} {:answer/id #uuid "f4274491-3f9f-5182-a334-44335bf752c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346618} :answer-field/id #uuid "13010a70-7fa5-5ef7-a02b-f50f4d30ab65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ce93ec7a-fcc3-5848-bb9c-a5b8e16bd97c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79353" :question/problem "Consider a $7\\times 9$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{rank}(A)=7$ then $\\text{nullity}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=9.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 7+\\text{nullity}(A) & =9 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =9-7 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348221 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5eb0fdb-7256-54c3-9a7a-a9dca48fc612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451741} {:answer/id #uuid "81ce3ea0-81f7-5844-b779-4c3cea386177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451742} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac36175b-5342-5095-991f-b0d75bb31fa9" :answer-field/key "$A$ is  **not** invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7348cde9-7949-5bff-82b0-8b60d1a6b39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451743} {:answer/id #uuid "01621a61-e00e-5295-bb23-ab9f96754ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451744}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451743} :answer-field/id #uuid "29d3a068-4e65-58a9-a0c0-45461411dede" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1ec200-5f46-55a8-a21f-13735ffe254a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451745} {:answer/id #uuid "6075a26d-50e6-543d-90ef-7a1fbac5e9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451746}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451746} :answer-field/id #uuid "12403c46-08ab-5006-baea-a3791d434532" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(A)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "322fb855-3d8d-59e6-ba9e-0f5421e84a86" :question/math-academy-id "q-79417" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix which has only $1$ non-pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{5} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=5=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is  **not** invertible” is true. Since $A$ has $n=5$ columns and $1$ of them is a non-pivot one, it follows that    \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Col}(A))=5-1=4\\ne 5.\n$$\n\n Thus, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is  **not** an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $2$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Col}(A))=\\text{rank}(A)=4$,  then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $4$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{5}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$ is true. Indeed, since $n=5$, according to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 4+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is  **not** invertible”; $\\text{nullity}(A)=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56bbd1f-d979-5241-8dc4-a8e64037e678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451747} {:answer/id #uuid "b5789825-466e-5d22-9f09-42c97e0435a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451747} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc17da3-e4d0-5047-880f-6b1aaa55e9cb" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(A)=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452721} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e85400e8-1b39-5381-89d4-9456a7b1822c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451749} {:answer/id #uuid "a330ce1f-b4c7-5d2b-9114-283fa3e27999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451750} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fca603b-0e04-57c7-b9ac-a845c8d3054f" :answer-field/key "$A$ is not  invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452722} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7bb58-5fc7-50bf-bcf6-b8a2a3b9a52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451751} {:answer/id #uuid "927e07de-2922-5088-8e17-5260e4060177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451752}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451752} :answer-field/id #uuid "6904f830-30a3-5787-9c3c-630ff0631353" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "251cb9e2-c851-5082-b166-e39206cbabf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79420" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix with only  $1$ pivot column?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{4} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=4=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ is false. Since $A$ has just $1$ pivot column, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=1.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not  invertible” is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=4.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$ is true. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 1+\\text{nullity}(A) & =4 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is not  invertible”; $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da20820a-7cf1-52ba-a9b8-2212f98fa16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451753} {:answer/id #uuid "7aefb362-63f4-504f-98b5-a2da25f151f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451753} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c53c77e-992f-545f-ba55-2353aade2814" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(A)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452724} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9882f7d4-51ae-5c58-b561-ff060c844fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451755} {:answer/id #uuid "2541f8cb-7e6a-5880-81b4-5ca6877fea1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451756} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6fdddfe-e700-5aea-b0ef-f2081cc85fef" :answer-field/key "$A$ is **not** invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452725} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "873e0c05-9845-5591-87a1-097a96eb6fbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451757} {:answer/id #uuid "5eba92ea-50a0-54c0-aba7-d88034bc70b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451758}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451757} :answer-field/id #uuid "c26a036b-3629-5b64-969c-37e14040c238" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "92ccb45d-8792-5a6a-9a10-b7498ac220cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-79423" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}?\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is }\\text{invertible}\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Col}(A) &=\\mathbb{R}^{4} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Col}(A)) &=4=\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\\\ \\operatorname{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\dim(\\operatorname{Null}(A)) &=0=\\operatorname{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)=2$ is false. Since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\right\\}=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=3.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+3 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is **not** invertible” is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=1\\ne 4,$ it follows, by the invertible matrix theorem, that the matrix $A$ is *not* invertible.\n\n- Statement “$\\text{Col}(A)$ is a $3$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}$” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=1$ then $\\text{Col}(A)$ is, in fact,  a $1$-dimensional subspace of ${R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is **not** invertible” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354635 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f2260d-7908-5a17-9d38-a9d0f295722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "754" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351894} {:answer/id #uuid "282dddef-1dc6-5f06-bffc-e9ed4063e18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-158" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351938} {:answer/id #uuid "3b8c6043-56a0-5050-9049-631d3e6340c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352116} {:answer/id #uuid "67e126ef-c44f-577d-99ef-b5c018ad680d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352493} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece99f2-c460-5d4a-a2ad-898a007dd715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352679}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352116} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4540e0b-356d-5436-a082-05ee75890a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "af3f4732-8a8f-51ae-b35b-754f1abeb181" :question/math-academy-id "q-88266" :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(B).$" :question/worked-solution "Given two $n\\times n$ matrices $M$ and $N,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(MN)=det(M)\\cdot det(N).\n$$\n\nIn our case, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}6\\sqrt{2} & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(24-(-5))\\cdot (-14-(-12)) \\\\  & =29\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-58.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47596227-58dd-5c85-b31b-dd2085e9d721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} {:answer/id #uuid "555a8298-192e-50be-8d44-14723e16f11d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352323} {:answer/id #uuid "58619be6-c805-5344-9021-173a95976dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352344} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9db913-3c22-5c37-be9e-ca617e5d0dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353213} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c2b5b7-b8a6-51db-98b0-9f4ba4bb301f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353585}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352226} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd53aab-78d5-531e-a248-9e6ce0d0938b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "31688145-09ed-5774-a522-d76e627c7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-88296" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=4,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $1$st row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & b+0 & c+0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}a & b & c \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{4}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354637 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a4042a-f605-574f-a228-ff4ae6d308e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352358} {:answer/id #uuid "73d447eb-1e81-51dc-b291-5725cee52d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352581} {:answer/id #uuid "74558c61-b294-5a8c-be71-44c0d3822c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} {:answer/id #uuid "b27eb1ec-b060-5c68-b2a6-225457f0de0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353093} {:answer/id #uuid "b5c62a94-26b3-5bea-ae7b-f2fbef1384ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352586} :answer-field/id #uuid "607dd21d-aeb7-5920-91bc-f439f202ce2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27889ca3-5f85-5eb8-aee5-93c6c3d6c2ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-88298" :question/problem "Given that \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b+3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}=5,\n$$\nfind \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We \"factor out\" the given determinant from the determinant we would like to find:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}a+1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ b & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, using the linearity property of determinants and expanding the second determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2)-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3)-3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ c+0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =\\underbrace{\\begin{vmatrix}(a+2) & -2 & 2 \\\\ (b+3) & 4 & 2 \\\\ c & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}_{5}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+1\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =5+(-10) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354639 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356b3816-6db1-52b5-bb62-b3c3cf31be2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "282" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352065} {:answer/id #uuid "4961badc-1952-50cf-a3aa-c27c2154e001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "447" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352242} {:answer/id #uuid "7e93fedb-8a75-5259-a81d-4a24a5c3ba66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "372" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352678} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b4f3fb-84a7-5faa-a662-e6383aedf972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353139} {:answer/id #uuid "d43b6429-3ee0-5e37-bfcf-0795e21063ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "327" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353406}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353139} :answer-field/id #uuid "d31711c3-cb15-50d5-862f-d3265b1300b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "26f8d807-4854-5fae-88e0-bb503698def0" :question/math-academy-id "q-88301" :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $2$nd row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 91 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90+1 & 92+0 & 93+0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 90 & 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{2+1}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 92 & 93\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(3\\cdot 93-6\\cdot 92) \\\\  & =-(279-552) \\\\  & =273\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2217be1b-0e43-59a5-97bb-6d402bea3ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "204" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351875} {:answer/id #uuid "74fa8be3-ab54-58c6-8925-9df3ccda76de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352594} {:answer/id #uuid "93ed11fc-9c76-519e-b094-03edef2b5efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352848} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f75795-6763-534e-888d-3664209ea4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "202" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352959} {:answer/id #uuid "c12ae922-eac4-5656-80c2-43cb7caa50fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353236}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352848} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a4bf2b-6495-570d-a5cc-83942fc77250" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da76c31c-efa0-5055-96f9-ec1ecc76273f" :question/math-academy-id "q-88304" :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st row of our determinant is almost equal to the $3$rd row. So, we can rewrite the given determinant, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}97 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, using the linearity property of determinants, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}96+1 & 98+0 & 99+0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first determinant must be zero since it has two identical rows, while the second determinant can be expanded across the $1$st row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}96 & 98 & 99 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 96 & 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} & =0+1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 98 & 99\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 99-2\\cdot 98 \\\\  & =396-196 \\\\  & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01175e40-f708-59a8-a68d-dddc912c5c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354663} {:answer/id #uuid "03aa0b9f-5ef0-5397-86ce-1bdf7e227448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 16\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354687} {:answer/id #uuid "3f434a6c-c8c5-5115-9380-3d6ac04f3b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355228} {:answer/id #uuid "5bee1569-0c1c-5f87-93cf-9d5d3f99b178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355476} {:answer/id #uuid "a914c905-27dd-5801-9326-3feb9816cdd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356162}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355476} :answer-field/id #uuid "97331fcb-ed96-54eb-be35-07c300d998b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "04c4a850-58dd-5ed4-a8bf-edb5b51c508e" :question/math-academy-id "q-91169" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{23}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{23}=-2$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 8 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 0 & 13 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 0 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 0 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-1)^{2+3}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 4 & 13 \\\\ 11 & 9 & 26 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "496fac11-29ae-50dd-b6d4-0f5fec7f1e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355313} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2f4e07-ac8f-539b-a082-42f26b6fc922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355612} {:answer/id #uuid "7b70ddaf-7367-5f2a-ac12-a6be8b4d9140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -2 \\\\ 10 & 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355749} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b27742-7bad-5cf1-9d73-f5ea224d0c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356353} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a806ad-64ab-57c6-98f1-817a816d783d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -3\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355313} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c888b6-8672-574e-a1e9-261cd3ebc924" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c88f672-e81c-5620-9a17-5b2addde6eb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-91196" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{43}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-4){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-3){C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & 6 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & 7 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & 6 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & 7 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{4+3}\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}14 & -20 & -21 \\\\ 17 & -31 & -23 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 6\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "068749ff-00c0-5411-89ad-26aee923eb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354711} {:answer/id #uuid "2847d2af-fd66-5233-908b-5eecb31cf46f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2 & 10\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355002} {:answer/id #uuid "2e98ac02-485c-5d18-b825-9f674d805616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355069} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ed764-d77f-5c1e-b981-5a63469e8c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355752} {:answer/id #uuid "a0767177-36f3-58b3-bda9-2a7cd1100697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356087} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36e522f-042c-5aca-9b90-23e00476716b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357323}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6989e3e-f986-5e0c-83b8-4e773e4738b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-91218" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{22}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+3{C}_{2} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+(-4){C}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $2$nd row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 10 & -10 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 13\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086baf34-04b6-58fc-b9f8-cd256a0f2e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354739} {:answer/id #uuid "3456bcab-7477-5733-9df3-89b18380c3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355127} {:answer/id #uuid "424cdac0-33e0-589e-94e9-d123e607def9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355250} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc929ce-9e29-5c94-bc6a-5cbf8f8914e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355906} {:answer/id #uuid "aebbe85d-89ff-5c2d-9a47-bea4603e6c6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355127} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a9facd9-8b32-5537-b94e-bb833971bbbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9a551105-1281-563b-9139-cdf718553556" :question/math-academy-id "q-91221" :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*" :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-4){C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -3 & 10\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
